items.text:
The rain has washed our footprints from the clay;
Her linen bears the cross she wrought in red;
Her sister drew the border round her head;
She died an hour before the break of day.
O Earth, her needle rusts upon the tray;
The last small knot is fastened in her thread;
Thou hast no task to set her in her bed,
Nor any wage, nor any debt to pay:
Thy matted roots are warm beneath the mould,
And, when the robin settles on her stone,
His breast will catch the winter’s level light;
Among the withered leaves thy winds have blown
A snowdrop rises in the keenest cold,
And bends above her with its burden white.

Be gentle, Earth, with her who comes to sleep;
Her weary hands lie folded now at rest;
Draw up the turf like linen to her breast,
And let no mole beneath her pillow creep;
Grow daisies where the mourners stood to weep,
And yew to stand against the windy west;
Suffer the wren to build her little nest
Hard by her head, and sing her slumber deep:
She hath no longer any need of tears;
She shivers not when winter fields are chill;
She counteth not the hours nor heeds the chime
That calls the village up at milking time.
Keep her, good Earth, through all the turning years
Until the last dawn kindle on the hill.

O Earth, lie heavily upon her eyes;
Seal her sweet eyes weary of watching, Earth;
Lie close around her; leave no room for mirth
With its harsh laughter, nor for sound of sighs.
She hath no questions, she hath no replies,
Hushed in and curtained with a blessed dearth
Of all that irked her from the hour of birth;
With stillness that is almost Paradise.
Darkness more clear than noonday holdeth her,
Silence more musical than any song;
Even her very heart has ceased to stir:
Until the morning of Eternity
Her rest shall not begin nor end, but be;
And when she wakes she will not think it long.

Thou hast her now, O Earth; her work is done.
Her needle keeps a little end of thread;
The faded quilt upon her narrow bed
Is warm beneath the pale October sun.
Her shroud is of the flax her fingers spun;
Thou hast a softer pillow for her head
Than those she sewed for hire to purchase bread;
Her wages cease before the week is run:
I brought her thimble, worn upon one side,
And hid it where thy broken clods were wet;
The wicket, when I turned, was open wide.
I brushed thy mould, O Earth, from off my gown;
A little in the hem is hidden yet
And I must take it through the lighted town.
